1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,375
A small team pays a dozen contractors every month.

2
00:00:03,375 --> 00:00:06,158
The invoices arrive as files and email.

3
00:00:06,158 --> 00:00:13,980
Someone copies wallet addresses into a spreadsheet, asks for approval
in chat, and hopes nobody changed a bank detail.

4
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:17,152
Ledgerly turns that into one verified batch.

5
00:00:17,780 --> 00:00:19,443
Late invoices are normal.

6
00:00:19,443 --> 00:00:27,445
Twenty-nine percent of freelance invoices are paid late, and
eighty-five percent of freelancers have been paid late at least once.

7
00:00:27,445 --> 00:00:29,339
The rarer mistake is worse.

8
00:00:29,339 --> 00:00:36,067
One wrong address, or one duplicate invoice, and the money is gone
without a receipt a finance team can use.

9
00:00:36,689 --> 00:00:37,927
This is the desk.

10
00:00:37,927 --> 00:00:44,655
The rules are public: a cap per payment, a cap per day, a lifetime
limit, and an expiry date.

11
00:00:44,655 --> 00:00:47,180
The payee book is the part that matters.

12
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:54,126
One name maps to exactly one address, and an address change has to
wait in public before it can take effect.

13
00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:57,957
Paste the month's invoices, or upload the files.

14
00:00:57,957 --> 00:01:00,765
Every line is checked before anything is paid.

15
00:01:00,765 --> 00:01:05,933
These three references already settled in an earlier batch, so the
batch is blocked.

16
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:09,103
No signature, no transaction, no money.

17
00:01:09,103 --> 00:01:12,918
The same check stops a duplicate hiding inside one batch.

18
00:01:13,549 --> 00:01:16,567
Changing an address is not a one-click event.

19
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:20,819
Choose the payee, enter the new address, and request the change.

20
00:01:20,819 --> 00:01:28,975
The old address stays the only payable one until the public window
closes, and a guardian can freeze the payee immediately.

21
00:01:28,975 --> 00:01:33,512
A hijacked email cannot redirect a payment that is already staged.

22
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:36,164
The owner controls the limits.

23
00:01:36,164 --> 00:01:40,171
Per payment, per day, lifetime, and an expiry date.

24
00:01:40,171 --> 00:01:47,002
The balance is separate from the team's main wallet, so a compromised
key can only lose what was deliberately funded.

25
00:01:47,002 --> 00:01:50,790
Revocation is one action and it takes effect immediately.

26
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:54,243
The contracts are tested, not just deployed.

27
00:01:54,243 --> 00:02:04,295
Thirty-nine tests cover caps, expiry, revocation, duplicate
references, frozen payees, and a changed address that must not

28
00:02:04,295 --> 00:02:06,898
One batch settled on the public testnet.

29
00:02:06,898 --> 00:02:11,717
The policy spent exactly one-tenth of a dollar, and the transaction
succeeded.

30
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:19,893
Every payout is an event on chain: the payee, the amount, the invoice
reference, and the transaction.

31
00:02:19,893 --> 00:02:23,991
The ledger is rebuilt from those events, not from the app's memory.

32
00:02:23,991 --> 00:02:29,107
Export it, hand it to the bookkeeper, and every line can be checked
against the chain.

33
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:30,549
Ledgerly.

34
00:02:30,549 --> 00:02:33,152
Pay the invoices, not the impostor.

35
00:02:33,152 --> 00:02:37,830
Live on Aevum testnet, open source, and ready for the next batch.

